class Counter {
    static int count = 0;
    Counter() { count++; }
}
Counter c1 = new Counter();
Counter c2 = new Counter();
System.out.println(Counter.count);
What is printed?

Given class A { void show() { System.out.print("A "); } }
class B extends A { void show() { System.out.print("B "); } }
class C extends B { void show() { System.out.print("C "); } }
What is printed by: A obj = new C(); obj.show();?

Consider the following code:
class Parent { static void print() { System.out.print("P "); } }
class Child extends Parent { static void print() { System.out.print("C "); } }
Parent obj = new Child();
obj.print();
What is the output and why?

